  "id": "COMPOUND:42636 - PROTEIN:5783",
  "verbalization": "Camelliquercetiside B is shown to have a direct target relationship with M19-MEL through multiple pathways, with the most direct link established via the MIP (methyl-19-oxo-14-yl-2,5,10,11-tetrahydroxy-6,9,13-trimethyl-12-oxo-7,17-dioxapentacyclo[11.6.0.03,9.06,10.016,19]nonadecan-14-yl) compound, which is itself a target of MIP. This suggests a cascade of interactions where Camelliquercetiside B modulates MIP, which in turn influences the activity of 2-Nonyl-4-hydroxyquinoline N-oxide, ultimately leading to M19-MEL as a downstream target. The evidence is consistent across three distinct pathways, each involving a different intermediate, but all pointing to a shared mechanism of action involving the M19-MEL target. The integration of these pathways supports a plausible, mechanistically coherent relationship between Camelliquercetiside B and M19-MEL, with the core mechanism likely involving the modulation of a signaling pathway that leads to M19-MEL activation. The evidence is robust, but the exact nature of the interaction between Camelliquercetiside B and MIP remains to be fully elucidated.",
